ncan is about valued our franchisees  understand that we've worked with them  very hard on pricing or what we call  smart pricing and yeah a couple of years  ago they may have gone too far on  increasing prices at restaurant level I  think they've actually they actually now  understand that that's not helpful to  driving  traffic back to your earlier question  and I think we're in for a good period  of time where we're not gonna see too  many price increases and that's going to  be good for our consumers and good for  our traffic nigel scarlett mentioned the  political situation and of course the  other thing that's happening in  corporate news and politics is  essentially the politicization of all  brands we saw during the superbowl we  see Trump calling out President Trump  calling out companies from time to time  it seems like despite the best efforts  of corporations they get sucked into  being on one side or the other how are  you thinking about that risk as a very  prominent consumer brand with a lot of  brand identity this risk that's out  there of your brands at some point  theoretically becoming politicized in  some way so Joe interesting question and  thank you for that I mean one of the  things I've tried to represent through  my time at Dunkin which is just about  eight years now is if we want to grow we  have to do it through our franchisees  and whenever we think about how we  influence administrations whatever  persuasion they are we have to do it  through their eyes so we try and stay  out of too much controversy we try and  make sure that our point is made we talk  to the administration we talk to States  we talk to cities all the time we take  groups of franchisees with us and and  basically our message is we need  policies that's good for small business  our franchisees are small businesspeople  we need policies for instance the tax  reform will be very helpful for all of  us and obviously sensible tax reform  immigration reform because one of the  things that our franchisees are  challenged with day-in day-out and they  complain all the time is fine in labor I  mean there is a little bit of a labor  shortage now which seems perverse after  some of the struggles over the last few  years we need some hold up on regulation  I mean I'll give you an example we had a  store that was being built in the  Midwest it was held up for over two  years because two water authorities were  arguing over who had jurisdiction over  that particular piece of land so I think  it's a lot of things we need  we try and be very practical we need to  be very we try and be straightforward we  try to be very civil you know we spend a  lot of time talking to administrators  right around the country so I think by  having good solid and sensible messages  that they understand we get our point  across well to that point that I wonder  what kind of impact have you already  seen on the president's immigration ban  how is it affecting your decision-making  right now at the moment  well Scarlett's good question we  actually checked on that we've actually  had none of our franchisees tell us  about any impact so far I I just think  that it's probably too early but we've  not heard anything from our franchisees  on it I want you to look back to a macro  question you mentioned the theoretical  the possible labor shortage out there  what is the situation in terms of wages  and how that's affecting franchise  emergence well well clearly there's been  some very fast moves to $15 in certain  states like California and New York a  franchisees have been concerned about  that but I don't want to give the  impression that they're anti the minimum  wage going up in fact we've always  encouraged very positive discussions  about that and we actually do believe  that increase in the minimum wage has  some kind of accelerating effect on the  economy but franchisees have been  concerned so we've worked with them as  many things we can do internally we've  looked at things like energy we look to  using labor more efficiently we've  introduced technology like our on-the-go  app is designed to actually reduce the  amount of discussion time between the  guest and the crew member we're looking  at streamlining our menu through a major  test we announced today but we'd also we  also think there's opportunities out  there sensible immigration return reform  is one as is trying to find ways to  recruit more younger workers because  youth unemployment is still above ten  percent and of course staying with the  macro idea you're expanding in China and  your big supporter of global free trade  despite what the president has said  you've said that you would give the  president concrete examples of the  benefits of being a free trade supporter  what is your most compelling  argument and have you made it to the  administration well we've not actually  sat down with the administration as yet  but I'm sure we will do we we had very  good discussions with the last two or  three administration so we'll find an  opportunity to do that yeah we do  support free trade we were in 61  countries I was in China Korea and Japan  three weeks ago they're all talking  about TPP they're actually talking about  taking advantage of the fact that  America is dropping out of that so it is  important to try and hopefully get rid  of some of these barriers so that we can  move products around the world  we actually have examples on the ice  cream side of our business the  baskin-robbins side where the more  countries we can get into will be good  for jobs in America because our ice  cream there is exported around the world  is actually made in Alabama so you know  we look forward to good positive  discussions about trade and obviously  trade isn't just focused on the US  there's some moves in Europe like brexit  in my original home country that we're  concerned about as well and we've made  our point on that very strongly not only  to the foreign secretary 